In article <e73480e2-6c43-4d3b-b6dd-df695f696514@b6g2000yqi.googlegroups.com>, jjh <jjhudak@gmail.com> writes:

> Things got a little more interesting with the vax architecture - Some
> of the 'modes' of address translation were, in my mind, somewhat
> convoluted. 

   On the original VAX 11/780, the floating point accelerator accually
   did much more than accelerate floating point.

   The FPA was inserted into the data path between the memory and the
   CPU, it would calculate results and feed the CPU instructions to
   store the result along with some NOPs to pad so that branches
   came out correctly.

   It also carried out many of those complex addressing modes.  So
   by the time the 11/780 CPU got them, instructions had a tendency
   to get significantly simplified.

   I don't know how many other models of the VAX implemented thier FPA
   this way, but I don't think these ideas were lost on folks building
   the high end systems.  (I was so disapointed when I found out that
   all the things I learned to speed up via unusual uses of MOVCx and
   POLYx on my 11/780 trapped to an emulation on my MicroVAX II and 
   became much slower than straightforward code.)



