>> And then there were the PDP-11s with no MMU - such as the one I have here!
>>
>> The first PDP-11 I ever used was a brand new PDP-11/20 - and that was 
>> only 16 bits, no MMU.
> 
>    IIRC, the 11/20 was a UNIBUS system.  The RAM capability may have
>    been too small to need an MMU, but the bus was 18 bit.  I had
>    an 11/34, same bus, similar limitiations.  And I had to deal with
>    all 18 bits since I was writing device drivers.

Correct. But if your machine don't have an MMU, you will only be able to 
address 16 bits anyway, even though the Unibus have 18 physical address 
bits.
The two high bits are permanently tied low, unless you access the I/O 
page, in which case they are forced high.

