>    All you have to do is tell the overlay supervisor to use memory
>    resident overlays.

La da di. Assuming you have a system which allows for memory resident 
overlays, that will:
1) Cause the same calls to the OS services I mentioned above.
2) Only work for code, not data
3) Cause overlays to be page aligned, which potentially could waste a 
lot of memory (one page is 8K)

>    Been there, done that.  And it was a major performance improvement
>    over a PDP-11 with an 18 bit bus but a small RAM capability where
>    we had to use disk resident overlays.

No denying it is faster than disk based overlays, but it is not a 
solution if you need more data space. Overlays is only a solution that 
helps you with more code space, by reusing the same virtual addresses 
for different pieces of code.

But I fail to see why people are arguing with me. All I am saying is 
that if a program needs lots of data space, it will become ugly. If 
other people disagree with me, fine. You write the program then. I can 
even provide you with access to a machine running the latest version of 
RSX-11M-PLUS, with all the tools you need.

