> 
> "ugly".....from an architectural perspective the MMU organization and
> operation is elegant (for the pdp11) IMHO.  The dynamic address
> translation is fast and actually has very little performance impacts
> (in general). Cache organization and reload policies affect the
> overall performance as well. In studies I did many many moons ago,
> performance impacts on address translation were minimal. It has been
> so long ago but there are many conference papers that did studies
> showing performance effects of various MMU schemes and coupled with
> cache schemes. Pullling in pages from disk at runtime is very costly
> but doing this at load time reduces the runtime performance hit quite
> a bit. Point is, from one perspective, performance was enhanced (pages
> already in memory, no disk load latencies), the time to do address
> translation is less than 1-2% of instruction execution time, hardly
> 'significant'.
> Things got a little more interesting with the vax architecture - Some
> of the 'modes' of address translation were, in my mind, somewhat
> convoluted. I'd have to digout some of my research papers to discuss
> specifics. The MMU 'complexity' got a bit out of control, partly
> because of the multitude of addressing modes of the CPU.  It has been
> argued that the VAX architecture 'went overboard' in the ISA, losing
> the simple elegance that the PDP family embodied.  The VAX ISA  was
> typically used  in comparison to RISC architectures  as a reason not
> to develop CISC machines....but those are arguments for another
> time....
> John

Sigh. Sometimes I wonder if people read what I write.
Thanks for a long text on another subject, and I totally agree with most 
you write. I know that the MMU design of the VAX was changed halfway 
through because the original design was too complex to implement, but it 
would have been a nicer design. But that's the compromises you have to 
do when trying to make a product...

But my comment about performance hit and uglyness was about the 
*changing* of the mapping in the program. When running under an OS, you 
cannot just go out and change the PAR when you want. You'll have to do a 
system call, which takes quite a lot of time. That time is actually so 
significant that DEC even implemented a special hack system call for 
just doing memory mapping changes in RSX (called fast map) to help 
improve this. But it is still quite a lot compared to any "normal" 
instruction.
And the uglyness comes from having to design a program to run on the 
PDP-11, when that program requires so much data that I actually do need 
to play with the memory mappings to get enough data space to do what 
needs to be done.

It has nothing to do with the PDP-11 architecture itself...

	Johnny

