>>
>>    If you don't know how to overlay your data, you don't know the
>>    overlay supervisor very well.
>>       Overlays only get updated on code reference, but you can, and we 
>> did,
>>    include data in those segments.
> 
> Yes. Readonly data. Not read/write. And that also assumes that you are 
> *not* using split I/D space.

Oh well, I should probably correct myself. If you have memory resident 
overlays, then you will probably be able to have read/write data in 
there as well. You just need to mix data and code in the same program 
sections, and the data will be located with the code, and mapped into 
memory along with the code. But it still means you have to not use split 
I/D space, and you are creating segments with a granularity of 8K, and 
you need to mix code in with the data in order to get the data mapped in 
automatically.

All this is in fact even more complicated than just creating a region 
and doing the memory mapping system calls yourself, that I totally fail 
to see the point of using the overlay system in order to try to use more 
than 64K of data. If using a memory region, and doing mamory remappings 
is what I considered complex and hurting performance, using overlays 
would make it even more complex, and you would have the same time 
penalities, since the same system calls would be used.

	Johnny

