I remember using FORTRAN IV with VIRTUAL around 1985.  The application used
around 1 MB of arrays in extended memory via the MMU.  Somewhere in the 
DECUS
stuff, there is alternative code which can use on-disk data instead 
which is a reasonable fit
when the memory is used as a small disk drive.  Under RT-11, the VM: 
device handler
worked well to reduce and actual disk drive's relatively slow transfer 
rates.

I know that FORTRAN 77 also had VIRTUAL arrays, but I can't remember if 
it used
the MMU or a disk drive.  This would be under RT-11.  Does anyone remember?

I assume that RSX-11 and RSTS/E had similar capability, although perhaps 
not under
RSTS/E with the RT-11 emulator using the MMU.  On the other hand, even under
the SJ monitor in RT-11, FORTRAN IV supported VIRTUAL with MMU, so perhaps
RSTS/E also found a way.  Does anyone know or care at this point?

As for the compression algorithm itself, can anyone provide some 
information on just how
that 128 KB table is accessed?  READ  ONLY?  Random?  Sequential?  Same on
decompression?  It would make a big difference if the access was 
sequential, but somehow
it would seem that it must be random access to that 128 KB table and 
maybe the table even
gets altered as the algorithm progresses through the input file?  While 
I am not about to
implement the code, it would help to know the characteristics of what is 
being discussed.

Jerome Fine


