> information on just how that 128 KB table is accessed?  
> READ  ONLY?  Random?  Sequential?  Same on decompression?  
> It would make a big difference if the access was sequential, 
> but somehow it would seem that it must be random access to 
> that 128 KB table and maybe the table even gets altered as 
> the algorithm progresses through the input file?  

For compression, it generates a tree structure based on what
it has seen so far.  Last time I looked into this was compress.c,
in the days before gzip.  If I put compress.c into google, the 
second hit, the one with actual source, comes from apple.com.
It is copyright University of California 1986, last changed in 1985.

It seems I wasn't quite right on the 128K.  The compression
table needs 2**BITS words, each of which holds a value between
-1 and 2**BITS-1, such that the usual implementation (with #ifdef)
uses (int) (16 bits on smaller machines in 1985) for BITS<16,
and (long int) for BITS==16.  One might be able to do that with
a 65536 entry table of 16 bit integers, and a 65536 bit table
indicating which entries where -1 to save memory.

There is even an #ifdef pdp11 which sets BITS to 12.

> While I am not about to implement the code, it would help to 
> know the characteristics of what is being discussed.

The basic idea is to build a tree structure based on substrings
previously seen.  If you find one again, point to the tree entry,
otherwise add a new entry to the tree.  Well, that isn't quite
right because the new entry, pointing to the previous substring,
is also added to the tree.  I think that means that for each 
character input you read one tree entry.  When you find a
substring not previously seen, write one tree entry.

For compression, there is not much reason not to use BITS==12.
The files will be a little larger, but the 16 bit decompression
program will still be able to decompress them.  The 12 bit
decompression program, though, won't be able to decompress
files with BITS==16.

-- glen


