I have made a suggestion which many may not appreciate for implementation
using a PDP-11 instruction set which runs under the Erstaz-11 emulator, 
normally
called E11 for short.

The available memory is limited only by the physical memory on the system.

Under RT-11 which has direct access to the IOPAGE registers, there is a DLL
called EMEM.DLL which allows the user to directly access the PC memory.
John Wilson wrote the original DLL which uses 4 IOPAGE registers to:
(a)  Mov  @#CSR, R0   /   Mov  R0,@#CSR     ;Access the PC memory
(b)  Mov  @#CSR+2,R0  /  Mov  R0,@#CSR+2  ;Also auto-increment next 2 
registers
(c)  Mov  R0,@#CSR+4   ;Set up low 16 bits of PC memory address offset
(d)  Mov  R1,@#CSR+6  ;Set up high 16 bits of PC memory address offset

I have enhanced the DLL so that:
INSTALL  EMEM.DLL  /SIZE:1024MB
can run under Windows XP on a system with 3 GB of memory.

Under Windows 98SE on a Pentium III with 768 MB of physical memory,
it is possible to request up to 600 MB for EMEM.DLL to use.  Not much
left for anyone else at that point.

It is also possible to use the MFPD and MTPD instructions which requires an
extra instruction to load or unload the stack, but which allows the user 
all of
the 64 KB for the current program and data, but direct access to the PC 
memory
via the EMEM.DLL with the use of the 4 IOPAGE registers.  The speed of the
access is slower than using normal memory under E11, but mainly because E11
is slower with the use of the IOPAGE.  The speed is mostly dependent on the
L1 and L2 cache sizes.  With a core 2 duo CPU (Intel 8200) with a 6 MB L2
cache, E11 seems to have little difficulty keeping almost all current 
code and data
in L2 cache.  If EMEM.DLL is instructed to request 1 GB of memory, the
access time will be much slower than if the data fits into L2 cache, but 
still
done at the speed of memory with no overhead needed for mapping as
with a real DEC PDP-11 that is restricted to less than 4 MB.

Most PDP-11 users are insistent that the code be at least able to run on 
a real
DEC PDP-11.  So the use of the EMEM.DLL would not be acceptable.  But
when I reach CPU speeds under E11 of 100 times a PDP-11/93 and disk I/O
speeds of 200 times any fast SCSI or ESDI drive on a real DEC PDP-11, it
is hard to resist running under E11.  For example, while it takes me 
about 240
seconds to compare 2 RT-11 * 32 MB partitions on the DEC PDP-11/83
that I still run on occasion, under E11 it takes less than ONE  SECOND.
Even on a Pentium III which I still use, it takes only 6 seconds.  And that
is about the only difference, aside from the noise of the 3 fans I use 
to cool
the 3 Hitachi 600 MB ESDI drives (DK515) on the PDP-11/83.  And
one other minor difference.  There are 6 VT100 terminals on my desk for
the PDP-11/83.  With E11, the ALT/Fn key selects up to 12 terminals to
be displayed.  And since I can't look at more than one display at a time,
it seems to work quite well with just the one monitor under E11.  Sorry, I
often get carried away.

>>While I am not about to implement the code, it would help to 
>>know the characteristics of what is being discussed.
>>
>The basic idea is to build a tree structure based on substrings
>previously seen.  If you find one again, point to the tree entry,
>otherwise add a new entry to the tree.  Well, that isn't quite
>right because the new entry, pointing to the previous substring,
>is also added to the tree.  I think that means that for each 
>character input you read one tree entry.  When you find a
>substring not previously seen, write one tree entry.
>
>For compression, there is not much reason not to use BITS==12.
>The files will be a little larger, but the 16 bit decompression
>program will still be able to decompress them.  The 12 bit
>decompression program, though, won't be able to decompress
>files with BITS==16.
