> called E11 for short.

Yes. And a program that can't run on a PDP-11, but only on E11 is, in my 
opinion, of little use to the PDP-11 community.

Why don't you just use the PC itself. That will allow your programs to 
run even faster, since you don't have the E11 emulation layer between 
you and the CPU?

And if I want access to large amounts of storage, I can just as well ues 
a disk. That will hold even more data than your memory device.
And then I can also make the program work on a real PDP-11.

Your solution would just create a stupid limitation that wouldn't help 
anyone.
Not to mention the fact that it is only useable on operating systems 
which allow you to manipulate the MMU yourself, which in practice means 
RT-11 only.

But hey. Don't let me stop you from writing a ZIP-program for RT-11 if 
you want to. But please don't bother me with it.

> It sounds like any one willing to make use of E11 can manage to 
> implement the
> algorithm under RT-11 quite easily.  I also wrote some F77 compatible 
> subroutines
> which aid the user to read and write the PC memory.  The subroutine 
> substitutes
> in-line code for the CALL to the subroutine after the first use.  
> Especially
> tricky to figure out was the subroutine which does a write to the PC memory
> since a normal F77 function CALL receives a value rather than doing a 
> write.

I think that if you want to go down that path, you should instead 
implement a ZIP-device. Pour the file into the device, and get the 
expanded results out. After all, you can then run ZIP on the PC itself. 
And ZIP have already been implemented. So such an exercise would not 
only be even faster, but it is mostly already done, so all you need to 
do is the actual file copying.
It will also be limited to E11 only, but then again, your solution is 
just as limited.

	Johnny

