glen herrmannsfeldt wrote:
> In alt.sys.pdp11 Jerome H. Fine <jhfinedp3k@dp3knospamcompsys.to> wrote:
> (snip)
> 
>> I have made a suggestion which many may not appreciate for 
>> implementation using a PDP-11 instruction set which runs under 
>> the Erstaz-11 emulator, normally called E11 for short.
>  
>> The available memory is limited only by the physical memory on 
>> the system.
> 
> Yes, there are always interesting things to do in emulation.
> 
> Back to VM/370 (The Virtual Machine system for S/370, which
> actually goes back to the 360/67) there is the use of the DIAGNOSE
> instruction to do operations outisde the virtual machine.
> S/360 and S/370 define a system dependent DIAGNOSE instruction
> (it actually doesn't have an assembler mnemonic) which VM uses
> for such purposes.
> 
> Are there any available opcodes in the PDP-11 instruction set,
> in case one did want to use a new instruction for emulation
> operations?

Sure. There are opcodes that are undefined, and which are trapped by a 
PDP-11.

>> Under RT-11 which has direct access to the IOPAGE registers, 
>> there is a DLL called EMEM.DLL which allows the user to 
>> directly access the PC memory.
> 
> I suppose the IOPAGE is where system dependent operations
> are supposed to be done on the PDP-11.

Well, you could say that. In reality, the I/O page is just a part of the 
address space where the PDP-11 don't have normal memory, but instead 
have devices.
You access them the same way as memory frmo the PDP-11, though.
So, for instance, to output a character on the console terminal, you 
write to the address where the console terminal controller is located.

Obviously, a sane operating system don't allow users to access the I/O 
page, since that would allow you to go around all the security and 
integrity of the OS.

And that is done by not having the I/O page mapped into the users 
virtual memory space.

	Johnny

