Talking of RSTS/E now, I assume...
You could not really pass that much data between different programs.
A CHAIN would wipe the memory, and as far as I can remember, files were 
not left open between programs. You you have the core common, in which 
you could save a little data between programs. About 512 bytes or so.
And that was it.
If you had more data, you had to write it to a normal file, which the 
program you chained to could then open, and read the data back from.
Pretty simple and obvious.

Virtual arrays in BASIC+ were also associated with a file.
Hmm, you could probably let that file survive across a CHAIN though, and 
in that way preserve the values (close file, chain, open file, dimension 
virtual array). Maybe that is what you meant?

