Reading through the F77 manual right now, it is a little unclear if 
multidimensional arrays are allowed to be larger.
It might be that you can create structures the size of 1M or more with 
multidimensional arrays...
I don't have the FIV manual nearby, so I can't check if it have better 
information, or other limits.

>> I know that FORTRAN 77 also had VIRTUAL arrays, but I can't remember 
>> if it used
>> the MMU or a disk drive.  This would be under RT-11.  Does anyone 
>> remember?
> 
> I can't say for sure how RT11 might do it, but both RSTS/E and RSX uses 
> disks for this, not MMU.
> And it would slightly surprise me if RT-11 had a totally different 
> solution.

Have to correct myself here.
BASIC uses disk based virtual arrays under RSTS/E and RSX.
F77 uses memory based virtual arrays.
The manual then goes on to point out that virtual arrays are only 
available on operating systems that support the Memory Management 
Directives. At that time, RSTS/E didn't, so this was RSX only (but then 
again, this compiler wasn't used on RT-11, so it don't really apply to 
that OS).
RSTS/E did get memory management directives later, so I assume that 
virtual arrays are possible there as well.

Anyway, so yes, they are allocated in memory, and you (the run time 
system) perform memory remapping as needed. Also, this means that one 
page in your virtual memory is reserved for virtual arrays.

>> I assume that RSX-11 and RSTS/E had similar capability, although 
>> perhaps not under
>> RSTS/E with the RT-11 emulator using the MMU.  On the other hand, even 
>> under
>> the SJ monitor in RT-11, FORTRAN IV supported VIRTUAL with MMU, so 
>> perhaps
>> RSTS/E also found a way.  Does anyone know or care at this point?
> 
> In RSTS/E it is disk based, not MMU.

Wong. In RSTS/E back then, no support at all. In RSTS/E now, memory 
management is used.

	Johnny

