>>>
>>> I find that rather unlikely. The array indexing is still just done 
>>> using a 16-bit integer, which limits the size of a virtual array to 
>>> 65536 elements. Even with INTEGER*4, that would only become 256K. 1M 
>>> is just not possible to address with virtual arrays in PDP-11 
>>> FORTRAN. Neither using FIV or F77.
>>
>> Reading through the F77 manual right now, it is a little unclear if 
>> multidimensional arrays are allowed to be larger.
>> It might be that you can create structures the size of 1M or more 
>> with multidimensional arrays...
>> I don't have the FIV manual nearby, so I can't check if it have 
>> better information, or other limits.
>
> Checked now (for F77). You cannot, in F77 for RSX and RSTS/E atleast, 
> have an array with more than 65536 elements total. No matter in how 
> many dimensions you cut it. So no, you cannot create a data structure 
> that is 1M in size under F77 for RSX or RSTS/E. I know that the F77 
> for RT-11 is a separate product, so maybe it behaves differently?

You might have missed the "s" at the end of the words "1 MB of arrays".  
There was more than
one array.  This was an FFT application and there was also a 
co-processor which did the
actual calculations.  So having special hardware is not new to the 
PDP-11.  Having special
features in E11 could be compared to having special hardware on a real 
DEC PDP-11.
Making use of these special features available under E11 would not be 
possible for those
individuals who make the decision to always use real DEC hardware.

As for the maximum index allowed in both FORTRAN IV and F77, I seem to 
remember that
under RT-11 implementations, a 16 bit index (INTEGER * 2) was not 
allowed to have the
high order bit on, i.e. FORTRAN treated them as signed integers rather 
than unsigned.  That
is not to say that an INTEGER * 4 variable could not be used in F77 
which would then allow
a value somewhat over 2,000,000,000 and still be positive, however, I 
don't think that F77
allowed the number of elements of an array to exceed a total of 32767 
whether multi-dimensional
or not.

I don't have the manual handy and, in any case, for something that 
pushes the limit of the
language, I would normally look at the generated code to check for myself.

As for the question about values which are still available for 
instructions, E11 specifically
has hooks which allow a user to write a DLL that handles that opcode.  
In fact, any opcode
can be handled by a DLL, already defined or not.  The DLL would take 
priority and execute
the instruction as per the DLL.  One point is probably helpful to know.  
Setting the condition
code bits after an instruction is emulated probably takes more of the 
effort than producing the
correct result for the instruction, especially since the X86 
instructions set rarely sets up the
corresponding condition codes.  But if the instruction does not need the 
condition code bits
set, then the emulation is rather easier.

In addition, the suggestion of using an IOPAGE register to trigger an 
operation is actually
very astute.  There are DLL hooks which are specifically targeted to 
handle the use of
IOPAGE registers, EMEM.DLL being just one example which can be 
downloaded from
dbit to have a look at the code.  It is quite possible for the DLL to 
change any value in to
PDP-11 registers or memory upon a reference to the IOPAGE register.  Any 
complicated
calculation could also be done.  While it would not be my first priority 
(unless there was
a very good reason to make it my first priority), an E11 emulation of 
that co-processor
that I mentioned which calculates FFT values from an array in virtual 
(extended) memory
would be very possible.

Jerome Fine


