In article <hrd749$37g$1@Iltempo.Update.UU.SE>, Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> writes:
> 
> I find that rather unlikely. The array indexing is still just done using 
> a 16-bit integer, which limits the size of a virtual array to 65536 
> elements. Even with INTEGER*4, that would only become 256K. 1M is just 
> not possible to address with virtual arrays in PDP-11 FORTRAN. Neither 
> using FIV or F77.

   You talking about a specific Fortran compiler's (or two) limitations,
   right?

   INTEGER*4 can reach values of over 2 billion (2^31 - 1).  F77 allows
   the user to specify the start index, so you could use all 32 bits
   (over 4 billion) to index an array.

   COMPLEX*16 uses up 16 bytes each, that comes to 64GB in conventional
   notation.  Fortran doesn't care whether the CPU can index using a 16 
   bit register, calculation the address of an indexed expression by doing 
   the math is a valid approach, and you can certainly manipulate 32 bit 
   integers on a 16 bit PDP-11 (I never saw a Fortran compiler for an
   -11 that didn't support INTEGER*4).

   Even using 16 bit indexes to COMPLEX*16 comes out to 1MB.

   I don't know what limits the F77 compiler you used on your -11 were,
   and you certainly can't address 64GB on any PDP-11 I've ever seen,
   but it's not Fortran's limitations.  Fortran can index all the RAM
   you could ever put in a PDP-11 (how wide was the SBI on an 11/70?),
   even if it might not be easy to implement (and I don't claim to
   know whether DEC did it).



