>   Fortran doesn't care whether the CPU can index using a 16 
>   bit register, calculation the address of an indexed expression by doing 
>   the math is a valid approach, and you can certainly manipulate 32 bit 
>   integers on a 16 bit PDP-11 (I never saw a Fortran compiler for an
>   -11 that didn't support INTEGER*4).

That may be, but as I understand it some compilers only use 16 bits
of those INTEGER*4 variables.

The Fortran standard requires INTEGER and REAL variables to be
the same size.  (For example, when EQUIVALENCE is used.)  That does
not happen by default for most PDP-11 compilers.  

I don't remember that I ever actually tested this.

-- glen


