Ah. Yes, now we're getting to the root of it all.
Your solution is indeed doable, and much easier. But that basically 
means that you will have an NFS file system on RSX, from which you can 
basically only do one thing: use a special program to read/write files, 
with possible conversions.
What is the point of that? That means you must definitely have local 
disk as well.

The possible extension that the NFS client also can play RSX-style 
files, under special circumstances makes it possible to use the NFS 
mounted disks natively, but you'd have to (as a user) almost know which 
files are ok or not, or else your programs just barf at you when you try 
accessing many files.

It's definitely an alternative solution. Worth considering... But for 
me, NFS looses a lot of its point if I am sortof incompatible with the 
rest of the NFS-aware world. I think it might even be difficult having 
two RSX systems talk NFS to each other with that solution (not that I 
see how any other solution would work either...).

