Yes. But then you have the question how you tell if a file is "native" 
to the client. NFS don't tell... And of course, a native file in the RSX 
case means we need information not possible to store in NFS, or Unix.

>>> The opaque model for NFS seems to indicate that your write data
>>> blocks to the server, and read them back.  Is this format only
>>> for text files?  So programs running on RSX can read disk blocks
>>> directly and process accordingly?  That would allow for conversion
>>> programs to be written for either side.
>  
>> Oh sure. If you aren't really interested in NFS as a file system, but 
>> just as a file transportation device, then sure. But why use NFS in that 
>> case. FTP will work equally fine.
> 
> Well, ftp still has some of the problems, but yes that does help.

Actually, ftp don't have these problems.
ftp was designed to walk around these exact problems. So, ftp deals with 
records, where each side are free to perform whatever stuff needed to 
figure out what a record is, how to store and retrieve it, and so on.
So for Unix systems, if you transfer in ASCII mode, a record is the data 
between two LF. The data is sent over, with the FTP definition of record 
terminator, and the other side then strip of the FTP record terminator, 
and writes the record to disk, with whatever tricks needed on that 
system (like adding an LF on a Unix system).
To, and from RSX systems in this case will obviously work just fine.
Binary files are sent without this transformation done, and things will 
survive fine as well.
You can even send special meta-information about files in ftp. Which 
means one RSX could send any type of file to another RSX system with all 
file attributes preserved. Some of those files cannot be transferred to 
another system at all, without corruption (well, you could probably make 
it compatible with VMS, so that they could interoperate correctly in 
this aspect, but not very many other systems).

This is a much more attractive problem to solve, since it is way more 
useful than NFS in my mind.

FTP and TELNET for RSX are the two most needed programs, in my opinion.
SSH would be nice, but realistically, the CPU is way to slow to handle 
the math to make SSH work.

