Well, that's not a file attribute, but yes. NFS semantics don't exactly 
match Unix semantics at all times. The same problems you see in Unix 
here are relevant for RSX as well.
(RSX semantics in how files live more match Unix than NFS.)

> I belive that another case relates to the read and execute bits,
> in that a file can have execute but not read access.  As the
> NFS server has no way to know what the client is doing with
> the bits, you can read such a file.  It seems, then, that one
> maps protection bits as well as one can, but not always
> perfectly.

The concept of execute protection don't exist in RSX. However, RSX 
differs betweem write access and extend access. Write access only allows 
you to write in the file, in blocks already existing in the file. Extend 
access allows you to grow the file.
And then you have delete access for files. Unlike Unix, where you don't 
ever actually delete a file. In Unix, you just unlink files, and when 
there are no references to a file, it is deleted. In RSX, you can have 
references to deleted file, and files can exist, even though there are 
no references to it. In Unix-speak, link/unlink are separate operations 
from file creation/deletion.
In order to "delete" a file in Unix, you need to have write access to 
the directory in which the file have a reference. In RSX you need to 
have delete rights to that specific file to delete the file. You need to 
have write access to the directory in order to remove the reference.

I can go on with more conflicting ways between RSX and Unix with regards 
to file protections...

And as I said, in RSX you have four groups, not three as in Unix.
You have SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP and WORLD. The system protection mask is 
applied to users who are in groups 0 to 7.

>> You could say that uids (in RSX) are gid local.
