> Even more, it assumes that there are UID and GID.  I believe
> that the PCNFS clients had special servers to get around that,
> I am not sure what Windows does.  (At least NT has user IDs.)

Yes, I was just pointing out that the way Unix and RSX use thses differ. 
In Unix you have the UID and GID. Which are two independen numbers.
In RSX you have something called a UIC, which consist of two parts. A 
group number and a member number. And they are connected.

>> Like I said from the start - I don't consider filename issues to be a 
>> problem. That part is easy...
>> But since you brought it up, I thought I'd just explain a little about 
>> how it works in RSX.
> 
> OK, I remember Radix 50 from RT-11, and sixbit from TOPS-10.

RSX use Radix50, though. Just to clarify. In RSX, a filename is 9+3 R5 
characters (4 words total). And then you have a file version number, 
which is a 5th word. (All words are 16 bits.)

And this in turn is then translated into a File ID, which is a 48-bit 
number, consisting of 3 16-bit parts, which is what all file operations 
are using when doing file operations. The FileID is very similar to the 
i-node number in Unix.

