>>> Among other features of OS/360 is locate mode I/O.  When reading,
>>> data goes directly into the desired location.  For writing, 
>>> the I/O system supplies a buffer address, data is written directly
>>> into the buffer, and then it is scheduled to be written.
>  
>> Same in RSX. It's optional for the programmer to do this, but it saves a 
>> lot of time not having to do the extra copying.
> 
> But only in 512 byte blocks, right?  The S/360 hardware formats
> the disk blocks to whatever size you want.  For RECFM=V, blocks
> are different sizes and there is a formula to tell you how they
> pack onto a disk track.  For example, the IBM 3330 track holds 
> 13165 bytes, with a 135 byte overhead (header) for each block.
> You can write one block of 13030 bytes, or 96 blocks of one byte.

Well, no. When you, as a programmer, request to read one record, you can 
specify if the data is to be moved into a second buffer in your program, 
or if you are just as happy if the code returns a pointer to the data 
inside the block as originally read from disk.
So either it is just one DMA, into your pogram, by the whole 512 byte 
block, but then you process the data in place in that buffer, or else 
FCS (or RMS) can transfer your current record to another buffer 
specified by you, on which you do your processing.

It's called locate mode or transfer mode.

And for both types, the operation also returns a pointer to the data, 
and the length of the data. So obviously, you can write your program to 
work correctly for both situations, and just flip a bit in the file 
operation call to get it either way.

Notice, however, that even if you specify locate mode, it will actually 
do a move for you, if the record you are about to access actually span 
several disk blocks. And of course, one attribute you can set when 
creating a file, is the information if it is acceptable for records to 
cross block boundaries. So if you really want fast access, you specify 
locate mode, and your files have the bit saying records are not allowed 
to span blocks.

> So far I have thought that an RSX based NFS client would be the
> most useful.  For NFS server, I suppose you would want to do that.
> It isn't likely that a unix based NFS server is going to implement
> special RSX features, though.

Both are interesting, in my mind.
But I would probably start with a client if I were to do this.

And no, any (other) NFS server is not likely to implement special RSX 
features. Especially since NFS don't seem to allow for special 
OS-specific extensions.

That is the same reason I see problems with an NFS client under RSX, 
which don't adhere to the Unix view when talking to the server, while 
presenting an RSX view to the local side.

