>> Well, no. When you, as a programmer, request to read one record, you can 
>> specify if the data is to be moved into a second buffer in your program, 
>> or if you are just as happy if the code returns a pointer to the data 
>> inside the block as originally read from disk.
>> So either it is just one DMA, into your pogram, by the whole 512 byte 
>> block, but then you process the data in place in that buffer, or else 
>> FCS (or RMS) can transfer your current record to another buffer 
>> specified by you, on which you do your processing.
>  
>> It's called locate mode or transfer mode.
> 
> I believe OS/360 assembler and PL/I both call it locate mode.

Slight error on my part. The mode where you don't move the data is 
called locate mode in RSX, but the mode where the data is copied a 
second time is called move mode, not transfer mode.

> I hadn't thought before about the possibility of doing it
> with fixed sized blocks.  It does seem that if you avoid crossing
> block boundaries that it works.  It seemed more obvious with
> variable sized blocks.

:-)
You can actually do it with records crossing block boundaries as well, 
with RSX. It's just that those records that do cross blocks will be 
copied anyway. But for those that don't cross block boundaries, no 
copying takes place.
The program can't tell which happened, without being more clever than it 
needs to.

