OK, NFS does everything by file handle which is 32 bytes (128 bits)
for NFSv2, and variable length up to 64 bytes (256 bits) for NFSv3.
It has to be big enough to include whatever the underlying server
file system needs, in addition to identifying the file system itself.
Also, I think they can change on client reboot (remount), so probably
they shouldn't be saved in other places.  

> This could be ugly... A program that want to open a file in RSX reads in 
> the directory file itself, and thereby locates the file entry, and the 
> corresponding file id. It then open the file, using that file id.

That sounds similar to NFS, though usually you do LOOKUP with
the desired name and the server does the actual search.  You could
read the directory, though, and pass that through.  You would then
need a hash table to convert between the 48 bit values and NFS file
handles.

> It can also store the file id, and reuse it later to open the same file.
> Also, there are a few file ids that are reserved, and essential for 
> anything to work, such as file id (4,4,0), which is the MFD (root 
> directory in Unix-speak). This is how a program finds a file. It starts 
> by opening file (4,4,0), and then traverse down directories from there. 
> (And yes, the MFD do exist in itself, normally under the name 000000.DIR)
 
> I would have to fake directory files for RSX, but I need to populate 
> them with filename, *and* file ids. And the file ids must then match 
> back to the filenames. I guess I could keep a translation table for 
> this, but that table could become very large...

Or, as I said earlier, map the whole file system onto one
big file in NFS.  Hard for others to read, but easier for the
client to do things its own way.

If I ever get to a CP/M version, that is the way the first one
will work.  That is the only way it can be done without changing
the BDOS.  (For similar reasons.)

-- glen 


