Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> wrote:

> glen herrmannsfeldt wrote:
<snip> 
>> I know about VMS zip, but have never used it.  
> 
> Used it, and it works the way you would expect and want. Files get 
> cipped/unzipped along with the VMS specific attributes.
> I haven't checked what happens if you extract a ZIP-file created on
> some other OS though.

I use it all the time; mostly between OpenVMS and Windows, occasionally 
with Linux or HP-UX. It's an excellent way to transfer files with VMS 
specific file formats across intermediate systems of all kinds; zip files 
are transferred as binary files, and the target OpenVMS system can 
restore the files with the original file formats and attributes.

When moving (text) files across OS boundaries one must take care as 
different operating systems use different formats for text files. If text 
files are zipped on OpenVMS without preserving the attributes, they can 
be used without problems on Linux/Unix as the files are converted to 
streamLF while they are packed. Windows has minr problems with this 
format as it expects <CR><LF> as a record terminator. Smart editors like 
UltraEdit compensate for this, however, and/or allow to create files with 
a U*X (and VMS) compatible format. Using ftp to transfer files 
uncompressed would also compensate for this problem (the ftp clients and 
servers do the necessary format conversion).

Packing e.g. PDF files on one platform and unpacking them on another one 
works flawlessly.

Hans.


