Hans Bachner wrote:
> [...]
> When moving (text) files across OS boundaries one must take care as
> different operating systems use different formats for text files. If text
> files are zipped on OpenVMS without preserving the attributes, they can
> be used without problems on Linux/Unix as the files are converted to
> streamLF while they are packed.  [...]

   For the record, any reasonably recent version of Zip
(version 2.31 (March 2005) or later) should let a user create
a Zip archive on a VMS system, saving the VMS file attributes
("zip -V"), and that archive should cause no problems on a
non-VMS system.  (Fixing some old bugs in that neighborhood is
what dragged me into Zip development.)  As usual, if someone
can demonstrate a problem, complaints are welcome.

   The files aren't really "converted to streamLF while they
are packed", but UnZip can generally do the job.

   Note that Zip offers options like:
      -l   convert LF to CR LF (-ll CR LF to LF)
and UnZip:
      -a  auto-convert any text files
      -aa treat ALL files as text
which give the user some control over how record boundaries
(including, possibly, line endings) are handled.


