> doubt anything looking for a TU-58 to read was going to go over
> 9600 or 19.2K.  And because there is no mechanical movement in
> the drive I expect it could shove data down the pipe as fast as
> the PDP-11/VAX could take it.  Actually, too fast might be the
> only potential problem!!

Since the speed of a DL11 is hardwired, no software ever really cares 
about what speed the serial port is using on a PDP-11. To a real TU58 
you of course had to limit yourself to what the TU58 talked.

With something else, you just have to limit yourself to whatever that 
other end is talking.

The big problem, though, is that this is no DMA device. It's either 
interrupt-driven, or polled. Either way it consumes a lot of CPU, and 
going up in speed is only going to make it worse. There is definitely an 
upper limit on how many interrupt per second a PDP-11 can handle, and 
each interrupt can only deliver one byte.

So no matter how high the speed of the serial line is, or how high the 
speed of the mass storage at the other end is, you will not be able to 
get that much speed on the PDP-11 side, and even so, it will consume 
lots of resources. And on top of that, a TU58 holds a ridicoulus small 
amount of blocks.

Not a good interface to emulate, except for the fact that you already 
have a device driver for it.

	Johnny

