> Well, the question wasn't so much a question of wether an RSX port 
> existed (which I doubt), but if you could inform a little on the memory 
> requirements of ZIP. How does it work? Does it create some large table 
> for compression purposes? I'm perfectly happy to work on an 
> implementation myself, if it's just realistic in memory usage.

Zip is mostly an implementation of LZW, though with the ability
to make archives (groups of compressed files).

The LZW compressor in 16 bit (usual) mode needs a 128K byte table.
The decompressor needs another buffer of similar size.
For compress (the unix LZW compression program) at least, you could
run with a smaller bit maximum, such that a smaller table was needed.
I am not sure that gzip/zip have that option.
 
> Somehow I doubt that the general implementation out there will suffice, 
> since I bet it's way too big to ever get to work on RSX, even though I 
> have a perfectly good ANSI C compiler.
 
> (Oh, and a VAX is not a PDP... :-)

Not a PDP, but still an 11?

