> 
> The Tu58 protocol is Radial Serial Protocol and the documentation is
> Currently Out There Somewhere.

Yes. And the original RSP had timing and flow control issues. Therefore 
DEC came up with the modified RSP (MRSP) later on, if my memory serves 
me right.

> TU58 performance had *two* limiting factors: serial line data rate
> (not brilliant), and seek time (verging on appalling).

Indeed.

> In the discussion here, which end are we talking about emulating? If
> you emulate the *drive*, you can create a "TU58" with zero seek time,
> and you *may* be able to make some other improvements too (serial line
> rate? number of blocks?).

I think the intention of the OP was to emulate drive and controller. But 
wouldn't know for sure, since it wasn't me, and the project is less than 
interesting for me personally, in any other sense than purely theoretical.

> In my younger days, I introduced my then employers to the delights of
> VT103s. A VT103 was a VT100 with a Qbus backplane inside (and a few
> other differences). You could get them with built in TU58s, or use an
> external storage device. We used them with assorted flavours of LSI11
> for RT11-based test equipment, whose typical mode of operation was
> "boot OS, load single standalone app, perform tests, log a small
> amount of data, print something to record the test results".

Yes. I know about the VT103. Have had a few around, but never actually 
ran any of them.

> RT11 on real TU58 was a bit slow to boot, but TU58s were dirt cheap
> and had other advantages (e.g. physical size - nil in a VT103) in
> comparison with the alternatives (e.g. RX02).
> 
> When RT11 introduced the concept of the LD (?) device, we took that a
> step further and implemented "RT11 clusters".
> 
> Each VT103 client thought its system device was a TU58. In actuality
> the "TU58's" serial line was connected to a DZ(V)11 port (or maybe
> DHV11, I can't remember 100%) on a "host" MicroPDP11/RT11 host running
> a home made application which had a separate LD device associated with
> each line on the DZ ie a separate LD device associated with each boot
> client.
> 
> An RSP request for a block would return the relevant block from that
> port's LD image. To make RT11 work on the boot clients, each LD image
> had its own RT11 system image. Job done.
> 
> The serial lines were still limited to DH/DZ/DL baud rates, but the
> horrendous TU58 seek times were totally eliminated and the time to
> application startup wasn't too bad (faster than a typical recent PC
> startup?).

That same solution have already been done several times. There exist RSP 
"servers" in the public domain. It's really simple, since the interface 
is a normal serial line.

And the server side, running on some kind of modern PC is way faster 
than needed anyway, so exactly what interface, and how it is used on 
that side is hardly relevant.

What matters is that unless you build a new device for the client 
PDP-11, you will have to use a DL(V)-11, which is the most stupid serial 
port for the PDP-11 ever invented. And not really a good thing to use if 
you want any real speed to talk about.

> The pseudo devices we used were not, to the best of my recollection,
> necessarily limited to the size of a physical TU58, although there may
> have been other limits imposed by constraints in Radial Serial
> Protocol (e.g. field sizes for byte counts). More than enough for RT11
> and a few apps though.

RT11 puts very little demand on the hardware. But most people will not 
be happy with a minimalistic RT11-system, but would want the extended 
monitor, lots of tools and programs, and so on. And suddenly it's not 
that fun anymore... :-)

> Because of the silos on the DZ/DH11 the "one character per interrupt"
> overhead didn't apply on the "host" (or "server") although obviously
> the client end still had a genuine DL11 and the associated overhead.

Well, not really true.
The DZ11 is close to one interrupt per character as well, even though 
the SILO can be emptied of several characters on one interrupt.
The same is true of the reception on a DH11. However, the DH11 use DMA 
for transmission, which starts making it a lot better.

But this is all server side, which I suspect would not even be a PDP-11, 
or anything even remotely related today, so that point is moot.

What matters is that currently, you need a DL11 on the client side. The 
DL11 is the real limitation here. If you build a new board, you can make 
it behave the same as te DL11 to the PDP-11, but be a lot smarter and 
faster talking to the server. That is what I think is being proposed, or 
toyed with.

> So, is that kind of thing of any interest to the current discussion?

I don't know. Others will have to tell...

