Bob Koehler wrote:
> In article <hr2d9r$lv$1@Iltempo.Update.UU.SE>, Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> writes:
>> The PDP-11 is a 16-bit machine, meaning a 16-bit address space. That is 
>> 64K (bytes).
>> On some machine, you can get away with 64K data and 64K code. But that's 
>> the limit before it gets ugly...
> 
>    I never had a PDP-11 with less than 18 bit address space.
>    Instructions could only access 16 bits of it at a time, but the
>    hardware was there to make use of the rest of it.

Yes... As you yourself note - you can only access 16 bits of it. The 
fact that the physical address can be more makes little difference. 
That's from the MMU, not the CPU. In order to play with a larger address 
space you either have to manipulate the MMU directly (not likely if you 
are using any OS), or else use OS services to change you memory mapping 
(assuming the OS even allows you).

And that is where it gets "ugly" as I put it. Big performance penalties 
involved, as well as becoming complex.

	Johnny

