t
> by overloading it because I know it is not that powerful. I suspect I cou=
ld
> have overloaded it by putting in a disk that draws too much power (could
> that do this kind of permanent damage?). Does anyone have any guidance on
> what limit I should apply to the power a hard disk uses when considering
> what disks to put in these machines?
>
> Thanks
>
> Rob

Rob,

Have you checked for dry solder joints? My recent issue with a 4000/60
power supply was down to a dry joint on the input transformer. It
manifested itself when I put a pair of 10k hard drives in the box, but
actually wasn't anything to do with them - probably just a mechanical
jolt which caused the issue. If you have a 'typical' drive for one of
those, say a RZ26L-E, then you should expect a draw of approx an amp
on each of the 12V and 5V lines - anything below that should be fine.

Regards, Mark.


