Bill Gunshannon wrote:
> In article <hps7i9$htt$1@naig.caltech.edu>,
> 	glen herrmannsfeldt <gah@ugcs.caltech.edu> writes:
>> PCI is likely somewhat harder, but allows for newer devices.
> 
> I think both of these probably go beyond the realm of possibilty for
> a hobbyist implementation.  By the time you got done the card would
> cost more than an entire PDP-11 or VAX system.

I would suspect that you have more or less complete chips, that aren't 
that expensive, that does the whole thing. But I haven't kept up to date 
on what chips exist, and how complex they are.

>>                                               You could connect
>> that to a SAN disk supporting your favorite protocol.
> 
> That would require the complete implementation of an IP stack.
> Considering the limited resources of a PDP-11, I would expect
> it to be nearly impossible.

Hardly. 2.11BSD already talks both IP, UDP and TCP just fine. Adding 
NFS, while some work, is definitely doable.

As for RSX, I already have IP and UDP running. Enough to implement NFS 
as well. There are another problem involved here, but capability wise, a 
PDP-11 will not be a problem.

> I would be more likely to look at a very simple ethernet level protocol
> that merely knew how to fetch and return raw blocks with a server
> process on another machine.  Or maybe that is something that could be
> done with LAT.  Is LAT what PDP-11's use to boot over ethernet?

No. LAT is for terminal traffic. Booting is done by another protocol, 
called MOP.

NFS, using UDP is not much more complicated than raw ethernet frames for 
reading and writing.
However, a big problem is that NFS don't give you a "raw" device. It 
actually understands various file operations, which means that if you 
have an OS with a different view how a file looks, or different views on 
file protection and privileges, which don't map to Unix, then NFS 
becomes a problem...

I've been considering implementing NFS for RSX, but so far I've decided 
against it for that very reason. There is no reasonable way to map 
between Unix and RSX, that is useful.

	Johnny

