In alt.sys.pdp11 Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> wrote:
> Bill Gunshannon wrote:
(snip regarding interfaces between Qbus and others, such as PCI)
 
>> I think both of these probably go beyond the realm of possibilty for
>> a hobbyist implementation.  By the time you got done the card would
>> cost more than an entire PDP-11 or VAX system.
 
> I would suspect that you have more or less complete chips, that aren't 
> that expensive, that does the whole thing. But I haven't kept up to date 
> on what chips exist, and how complex they are.

I might not know Qbus quite well enough, but I think it should
easily interface to ISA.  The interface might need to know which
addresses to forward, though.

For PCI there are chips, but they might need some programming.
 
>>> You could connect
>>> that to a SAN disk supporting your favorite protocol.
 
>> That would require the complete implementation of an IP stack.
>> Considering the limited resources of a PDP-11, I would expect
>> it to be nearly impossible.
 
> Hardly. 2.11BSD already talks both IP, UDP and TCP just fine. Adding 
> NFS, while some work, is definitely doable.
 
> As for RSX, I already have IP and UDP running. Enough to implement NFS 
> as well. There are another problem involved here, but capability wise, a 
> PDP-11 will not be a problem.
 
>> I would be more likely to look at a very simple ethernet level protocol
>> that merely knew how to fetch and return raw blocks with a server
>> process on another machine.  Or maybe that is something that could be
>> done with LAT.  Is LAT what PDP-11's use to boot over ethernet?
 
> No. LAT is for terminal traffic. Booting is done by another protocol, 
> called MOP.
 
> NFS, using UDP is not much more complicated than raw ethernet 
> frames for reading and writing.
> However, a big problem is that NFS don't give you a "raw" device. It 
> actually understands various file operations, which means that if you 
> have an OS with a different view how a file looks, or different views on 
> file protection and privileges, which don't map to Unix, then NFS 
> becomes a problem...

You can cheat.  Map the whole file system to one big file on
the NFS server.  Not so convenient from the other side, but it
should be fine for the PDP-11 side.  That would be similar to the
way that diskless unix machines swap on NFS.  

Not so convenient in that it doesn't allow one to work with 
files from other systems, but I think it is all you can do from
the device driver level.  Otherwise, it has to be done higher
in the file system code, much higher than the device driver level.

It is interesting that in NFS the client works at a higher level,
and the server at a lower level.  On unix, for example, if you
mount a disk on a mount point then other files in that part of
the directory tree aren't visible anymore.  If you mount that
file system through NFS then those files are visible!  
 
> I've been considering implementing NFS for RSX, but so far I've decided 
> against it for that very reason. There is no reasonable way to map 
> between Unix and RSX, that is useful.

I did use RSX some years ago, but maybe don't remember it so well.
Which part doesn't map well?  It does have to be done at a higher level.

I remember early versions of HP-UX, based on the AT&T unix systems,
had a short file name limit for native file systems.  That limit was
not there for NFS mounted file systems.  To do NFS right, it really
does need to be done at as high a level as possible.  Among others,
the file name length limit should also disappear for RSX.  
(Possibly a problem for programs without a big enough buffer for
a file name input.)

-- glen


