Q-bus ==> Storage...

For my few cents worth of thinking, I'd say that a MSCP implementation
done in "public space" would be great!

As I have understood it, many PGA-designs etc include a CPU-core, with
embedded "ROM" and "RAM" as part of the programmed array. This is, as
a concept, identical to the slightly older implementation using MSI/
LSI-scaled chips and a separate "embedded controller".

After reading the quite relevant book "The SCSI Bus and Ide Interface"
- that I really recomende reading before finalizing this design - I
have learned that the IDE interface is just a "recode" of the ST-506
commands directely over the x-86 system bus, as opposed to SCSI which
is at a higher level of abstraction. In other words, with a SCSI-disc,
the controller/driver SW is offloaded quite some fuzz with records
placement, bad block handling etc.

So, for my opinion, count one more pro SCSI.

But for future, one migth concider SAS (though not for free as today)
or cheaper SATA, that is said to be actually a mixture of ATA
interface and SCSI-commands using serial connectors... -I have not
checked up on this myself, though.

The paralell ATA/IDE interface is quite close to the xxx86-system bus
of the same years (8086? / 80286??). So, using a x86 core or chip for
a controller would most likely make it relatively easy to interface
the IDE disc "outside", and require to program the MSCP-part towards
the PDP, though "by religion", I'd prefer using either a PDP or some
68...-technology for the interface computer/core.

Using such a technique, the next thought in my brain is a USB-memory
stick, and when that is viewed, an USB-connected HD comes fast in
mind...   ATAPI-CD's,   which is just a way of running CD SCSI-
commands using IDE-interface might be of some interest.

SD-card, hmm, it is used in cameras etc, but for primary storage !??
USB sticks are "for free" at my local low-cost supermarket...

As for thinking ethernet, why has noone said I-SCSI   ??? This would
be quite modern thinking, woulde't it? Or would FcOE (Fibre-Channel
over Ethernet) be simpler to implement? (I-SCSI seems to require a TCP-
IP stack inside the "interface processor", as opposed to FcOE that
only uses "low-level" Ethernet to transport Fibre-Channel commands.
I'm however not familiar with if there are any available FCoE-servers
to be run in, say Free-BSD or not.

I'm today (commersially) operating a number of 11/93's using SCSI-
discs, placed all around a dev. laboratory. I'd love to replace all
these discs with a controller and an extra Ethernet-cable (100M/1G)
connecting using a separate storage network to some kind of storage
inside a computer room, instead of having discs in each computer. This
controller card could, in my case, speak "SCSI as a disc", but going
straight for the bus would be still nicer.

One neat thing of implementing this as a core and some "fw" inside
some gate-array-logic would be the possibility to include a PDP (with
memory etc) in other parts of the same logic device. That would give a
PDP with disc-interface included "on a chip"!

The same chip should better include an ethernet interface, one or a
few serial interfaces, maybe an vt300 or better terminal (using some
PC-screen & KB) usable as a console. To me, paralell to the physical
disc-interface there could be a separate ethernet and USB (or fire-
wire?) for storage purpuses.

This chip should use some kind of "bus" internally, and to any
"external" device (that could be a Q or UNI-bus physical interface or
an emulated device again!). Such a bus would be very nice to unite /
standardize upon, to that several projects actually could be
connecting each other!


For oher ideas of storage; for the low-end, have you concidered to
implement some decent HIGH SPEED serial interface in the Q-bus
machine, and a responding TU-58-emulator? That would go underneeth any
MSCP-thinking, but, depending on speed achieved, might give some bucks
of storage technolgy for just an handful of dollars!

Happy designing!

/G|ran ]


