Johnny Billquist  <bqt@softjar.se> wrote:
>Rob Jarratt wrote:
>> There was some discussion recently about creating an FPGA-based QBUS 
>> card with an SD interface which could then be programmed to emulate any 
>> disk you might be interested in. Of course the programming would not be 
>> trivial (I don't know anything about FPGAs, yet). I would still be 
>> interested in this project as I want something that looks like an RD54 
>> (I only have a single functioning RD53).
>
>The thing is, you will not emulate an RD54. You will "emulate" MSCP. And 
>MSCP itself allows for disks of just about any size, so once you have 
>done this, you might as well go for the full monty instead of just 
>pretending to be an RD54.

Nahh, the idea is you emulate the RD54.  You get your disk, put on the
board, set the board jumpers for "IDE RD54" and plug it into the RQDX3.

So you keep the existing interface... all the buss interfaces are being
done by the RQDX3 so you don't have to worry about that... you just emulate
the IDE disk.

The advantage here is that you can ALSO use the same board on a CP/M machine
that takes IDE disks.... or on a Data General machine that takes IDE disks,
or on a lot of other machines, just by moving jumpers around.

>The RQDX controller was just DECs MSCP controller for IDE disks, and the 
>IDE side was rather limited. From the PDP-11 it's just MSCP though, and 
>very much more unlimited.
>
>But MSCP is a complex protocol for a disk interface. Not saying that it 
>can't be done, but it is not trivial.

You don't implement MSCP.  It's complex, and it's hard to do, and once you do
it, you have a device that you can use only on DEC machines.

>(MSCP controllers exists for IDE, SCSI, SDI, DSSI, and probably other 
>disk interfaces as well.)

The problem is that RQDX3 controllers are very common and cheap, and the 
SCSI controllers are somewhat rare and expensive.  And a lot of the SCSI
controllers you find out there are tape-only unless you upgrade the roms.
If someone was selling cheap QD33 cards brand new, there would be no
need whatsoever for IDE emulation, at least for vax and pdp-11 stuff.  But
they aren't.

And don't forget that the Decmate II users need IDE drives too!
--scott
