In alt.sys.pdp11 Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> wrote:
(snip about NFS where I wrote)
 
>> That, and that it lets you see any files that could be under
>> the mount point.
 
> Part of the fact that you export disks, and not file systems, yes. The 
> files "hidden" by a mount point are still there on the disk.
> It might seem a bit weird if you look at it from a certain angle, 
> but it makes perfect sense from another angle. :-)

But also it is where you see that an NFS server works at a lower
level, in terms of file systems, than the client.

(someone wrote) 
>>>>> I've been considering implementing NFS for RSX, but so
>>>>> far I've decided against it for that very reason. 

(snip of discussion of RSX filesystem data separate from the file)
 
>> A problem well known in the Apple world.  In Macintosh file systems,
>> files have a data fork (the normal part) and resource fork
>> (where data like you mention goes).  It is normally handled in
>> NFS, or on other file systems, with an extra file somewhere.
>> The ones I remember put an extra subdirectory in each directory,
>> sometimes with a hidden name (such as .RESOURCE_FORK) and put
>> the data in there.  Not great, but it does work.  It could
>> also be done with another file in the same directory with a
>> different name.
 
> Ugh! Hmm, yes, that could be doable, but definitely not a nice 
> or neat solution.
 
> It runs the risk of people messing with those "extra" directories 
> and files on the other end.

Well, the client (on said system) could block access to the special
directories or files.  They would be visible on other systems, though.

In the NFS file system model, section 1.4.3 of NFSv4:

  "The server filesystem is hierarchical with the regular files
   contained within being treated as opaque byte streams."

It seems that NFSv4 allows UTF8 coding for file names, but file
data is considered opaque (to the server).  

From the NFSv3 specifications: 

  "NFS servers are dumb and NFS clients are smart.  It is the clients
   that do the work requried to convert generalized file access that
   the servers provide into a file access method that is useful to
   applications and users."
 
>>> Second, depending on the contents of that data, the file is processed 
>>> differently. For example, a normal text file in RSX is actually 
>>> represented by a 2-byte length field, followed by the data. No CR or LF 
>>> is included in the record. And records are aligned on even addresses.
>>> So, if you read a text file over NFS, you need to fake something for the 
>>> meta-data, and then you need to read the lines from the file, and at 
>>> each LF, you need to split the data up.
 
>> I believe that there are systems that convert between LF terminated
>> and CRLF terminated records while doing NFS I/O.  Not that I would
>> do that, though.
 
> Hmm. It would be nice if you could name a system that do, so that I 
> could check it out.

I have used two NFS implementations for DOS/Windows systems.
PCNFS from Sun, in the MSDOS days, and SFU3.5 (services for unix)
written by Interix and available free from Microsoft.  
(It works with Win2K, XP, and Server 2003.)  I believe both support
client side (and mount time option) to convert between CRLF and LF
terminated lines.  Many systems, such as current Linux, include
the unix2dos and dos2unix programs to convert to/from CRLF 
terminated lines.

Presumably such mount points should not be used for executable
files, unless the NFS client can figure that out and not translate
them.  Also, the conversion is also needed, for example, if 
files are transfered through tape, direct mounting of disks
(such as USB drives), or other file transfer methods.
 
>>> However, NFS don't actually read a record at a time, but reads 
>>> a disk block at a time. 
 
>> I believe that the client can read any size desired up to the
>> server specified maximum.  Commonly that would be a multiple
>> of the disk block size, but I don't believe that is required.
 
> I was pretty sure it was disk blocks. Yes, you can specify that it 
> should be multiples of 512 byte blocks, but that's just for higher 
> throughput. NFS will adjust to the lower value of the two sides when 
> client and server talk.

The NFS READ and WRITE requests take a byte offset and byte
length specifier.  There are complications due to EOF, which it
seems where improved in NFSv3, but at least back to NFSv2 they
are specified at byte boundaries.  
 
> Well, I might be wrong, and if I am, that would be great.
 
>>> And with each line split, the LF removed, and 2 bytes 
>>> of length added, plus a possible padding byte, means that a disk block 
>>> from NFS will end up being slightly more that one disk block under RSX. 
>>> And of course, the opposite needs to be done when writing.
>>> And you don't even know if it actually is a text file, for which this 
>>> should be done.
 
>> The client should know that an process accordingly.
 
> The client would need to know, no matter how. The point is that the 
> length of the file possible changes as an effect, and by how much is 
> impossible to know except by processing the whole file. And if I'm right 
> about blocks, it would introduce an extra level of buffering, since 
> you'd need to repack disk blocks, and one block access might result in 
> two accesses to the other end, and so on.

As above, any such conversions are done by the NFS client.  

It seems that IBM has gone through this with z/OS (previously MVS)
and NFS.  See: httpL//publibz.boulder.ibm.com/epubs/pdf/cpn1ug00.pdf

MVS has three different basic file record formats (RECFM),
RECFM=F (fixed length), RECFM=V (variable length), and RECFM=U
(undefined).  For RECFM=F, and especially for FB (fixed blocked)
all records are the same length, stored in the directory (TOC),
with no record marks stored in the file.  For RECFM=V (and VB)
records and block have four byte headers indicating the lengths.
RECFM=U (commonly used for executable files) the structure is
transparent to the file system, but known to the client.
 
>>> And changing the lengths also mean that any seeks cannot be 
>>> converted at all, since you don't know where that actually is 
>>> on the file because of record length differences.

Yes this problem is well known, even for text files in standard C.
Note the restrictions on fseek/ftell.  
 
>> Personally, I believe that you shouldn't change the length.
>> Write the bytes as seen by the client, and leave it up to other
>> systems to process accordingly.  This problem should also occur with
>> some IBM systems, where record structure is not stored in the file,
>> but otherwise known.  I haven't used NFS on such systems to know
>> how it is done, though.
 
> Ok. So totall non-interoperability over NFS. What should I do with files 
> that were created on one side, when they are accessed on the other side?
> Pretend they don't exist? That can cause interesting problems of its own.

It seems that there are some "pretend they don't exist" problems
already.  One comes with case sensitivity.  If the server has two
files with names that differ only in case, how can they be
accessed by a case insensitive client (DOS/Windows or Macintosh,
including OS X)?  Or the name may be too long for the client.

It seems now that with 64 bit NFS, allowing files longer than 4GB,
and 32 bit clients, that can't process such files, that again
files pretend not to exist.  In the spec. they even note that this
can happen while a file is open, at it crosses the 4GB boundary!

> Pretend they are fixed length files? But NFS (and Unix) measure file 
> length in bytes, while RSX measure it in blocks. Or should I pretend 
> they are STREAM files? Which makes them rather difficult to play with, 
> but which might be more approriate, except when we really talk about 
> binary files, which might make sense to say that they are fixed length 
> files.

The opaque model for NFS seems to indicate that your write data
blocks to the server, and read them back.  Is this format only
for text files?  So programs running on RSX can read disk blocks
directly and process accordingly?  That would allow for conversion
programs to be written for either side.
 
>>> I could go on about binary files as well, where you can hit 
>>> the same problems...  
>>> And then we have things like owners and file protections, 
>>> which don't map right...

Owner and file protection is also well known.  (Especially for
DOS which has none of either.)  In some cases special servers
exist to do the conversion, but otherwise NFS works with numeric
UID and GID and the usual unix rwx protections.  As well as I
remember, the rwx idea came from DEC systems, so might work 
with RSX.
  
>>> In VMS, this is solved by forcing files on NFS to always have the 
>>> STREAM_LF format. That is a possibility under RSX as well, since you do 
>>> have a STREAM format for RMS. However, FCS have no clue about that 
>>> format, and just about no program can deal with it. So it wouldn't be 
>>> very useful.

I don't remember ever using NFS on VAX.  I will have to get
my VAX/VMS system running again.
 
>> Well, I was suggesting it mostly such that it would only ever
>> be read by the RSX system.  That is, as a replacement for older
>> disk hardware.  It would be nice to be able to read/write from
