It's not the client side that worries me here, but the server side. 
Those files will be native files and directories on the server, meaning 
you can access them directly there, and mess things up when the client 
then use them.

> In the NFS file system model, section 1.4.3 of NFSv4:
> 
>   "The server filesystem is hierarchical with the regular files
>    contained within being treated as opaque byte streams."
> 
> It seems that NFSv4 allows UTF8 coding for file names, but file
> data is considered opaque (to the server).  

UTF-8 is a totally different issue, which have nothing to do with this. 
UTF-8 is just a way of coding Unicode into 8-bit bytes. If your 
filenames consist of 8-bit bytes, and all values are allowable, then you 
can parse your filename as UTF-8, if you wish to do so. You can also 
parse it as LATIN-1, and get a different file name for the same sequence 
of bytes. It's all just a question of interpretation, and not something 
the file system have any clue about how you do/want to do.

As for my "problem", the quoted paragraph pretty nicely wraps it up. 
Files are treated as opaque byte streams. In RSX, files are not opaque 
byte streams...

> From the NFSv3 specifications: 
