>> client and server talk.
> 
> The NFS READ and WRITE requests take a byte offset and byte
> length specifier.  There are complications due to EOF, which it
> seems where improved in NFSv3, but at least back to NFSv2 they
> are specified at byte boundaries.  

Ok. I had to read through the NFS rfc. Yet are right, the operations are 
not on blocks, but bytes. However, that helps nothing. What would have 
helped would have been if they had operated on records...

In RSX, basic operations are done on blocks. And a block is 512 bytes 
when we talk disks.
However, each program has then a library which deals with the unblocking 
of data, unlike in Unix, where the kernel does this.
So, user applications always expects to see raw disk blocks, with the 
record information in them. And then the user program deblocks the data, 
presenting a record at the time to the application. This code is eoither 
FCS or RMS in RSX, and it's a library that you can either link into your 
own code, or else use as a shared library. This code is the part that do 
all the filename parsing, the directory lookups, directory 
manipulations, and so on. And this code also is the one managing the 
meta-data for the files, and decide how to interpret the contents of the 
file, based on the meta data.

So, if you read a text file from Unix, and expect to present it to an 
RSX application, you need to read some (unknown) amount of data from the 
Unix server, pack this into a 512 byte block for the RSX operation, with 
record lengths between the record of data, and padded appropriately, and 
pass that 512 byte block to the user application.

And the exact offset from which to read is also unknown, except for 
offset 0.

>>>> And with each line split, the LF removed, and 2 bytes 
>>>> of length added, plus a possible padding byte, means that a disk block 
>>>> from NFS will end up being slightly more that one disk block under RSX. 
>>>> And of course, the opposite needs to be done when writing.
>>>> And you don't even know if it actually is a text file, for which this 
>>>> should be done.
>  
>>> The client should know that an process accordingly.
>  
>> The client would need to know, no matter how. The point is that the 
>> length of the file possible changes as an effect, and by how much is 
>> impossible to know except by processing the whole file. And if I'm right 
>> about blocks, it would introduce an extra level of buffering, since 
>> you'd need to repack disk blocks, and one block access might result in 
>> two accesses to the other end, and so on.
> 
> As above, any such conversions are done by the NFS client.  

Exactly. And that means the code inside RSX. Which needs to then read 
through the whole file to calculate the actual length of the file, once 
the conversions have been done, in order to then be able to give a 
correct file size, and end pointer to the user application. That means a 
lot of work just because you open the file. Meaning very slow, and 
reading through a lot of file for nothing. On the client side. Which 
also means a lot of network traffic.

> It seems that IBM has gone through this with z/OS (previously MVS)
> and NFS.  See: httpL//publibz.boulder.ibm.com/epubs/pdf/cpn1ug00.pdf

Ah, yes. Reading through that paper now.

And they have to deal with exactly the same issues, and the solution is 
the same. For the server side, serving from VMS to Unix systems, they 
have to linearly read through a file to find the correct point, since 
they also deal with records, and no streams of bytes.
In that case, the intelligence have to be in the server, since it's the 
z/OS responsibility to make it look the same way as a Unix file, even 
though the backend store is not.

And the same goes in reverse too. It is the z/OS client which have to 
serialize/deserialize data and so on, to adopt to the stream byte view, 
while the application locally wants to play with records...

I causes serious performance problems, as well as being lots of code.

I'm not saying it can't be done, just that I'm not to keen on 
implementing this all.

>>>> And changing the lengths also mean that any seeks cannot be 
>>>> converted at all, since you don't know where that actually is 
>>>> on the file because of record length differences.
> 
> Yes this problem is well known, even for text files in standard C.
> Note the restrictions on fseek/ftell.  

Yes. But the fact that the problem is known will in no way make it solved.
Let's play with the fseek/ftell here for a second. C stipulates that you 
can only do fseek to a value previously returned by ftell.
So, we have a (two actually) C compilers on RSX. You you call ftell in 
them, you�ll get some value back, which is based on what FCS thinks. 
This will basically be a value telling a block, and a byte offset within 
that block, to which you then can do an fseek.
Assuming we were to use NFS here, you would still get such a coded value 
by ftell. When you then call fseek, you'd be passing that same value in 
again. Giving a disk block, and an offset within that disk block where 
the record starts.
However, the file, served over NFS don't actually look like this. It is 
a stream of bytes, remember? So you would have to start reading from the 
beginning of the file, catching each LF, mark that as a record 
separator, round up your position to where the next record would start, 
and keep counting until you hit the right disk block, and right offset, 
when (hopefully) and record would begin.

All to fulfill the very restrictive definition on how fseek/ftell are 
assured to work in C.

>>> Personally, I believe that you shouldn't change the length.
>>> Write the bytes as seen by the client, and leave it up to other
>>> systems to process accordingly.  This problem should also occur with
>>> some IBM systems, where record structure is not stored in the file,
>>> but otherwise known.  I haven't used NFS on such systems to know
>>> how it is done, though.
>  
>> Ok. So totall non-interoperability over NFS. What should I do with files 
>> that were created on one side, when they are accessed on the other side?
>> Pretend they don't exist? That can cause interesting problems of its own.
> 
> It seems that there are some "pretend they don't exist" problems
> already.  One comes with case sensitivity.  If the server has two
> files with names that differ only in case, how can they be
> accessed by a case insensitive client (DOS/Windows or Macintosh,
> including OS X)?  Or the name may be too long for the client.

Indeed. But should I do that for *all* files, just because I might have 
to do it for some?

>> Pretend they are fixed length files? But NFS (and Unix) measure file 
>> length in bytes, while RSX measure it in blocks. Or should I pretend 
>> they are STREAM files? Which makes them rather difficult to play with, 
>> but which might be more approriate, except when we really talk about 
>> binary files, which might make sense to say that they are fixed length 
>> files.
> 
> The opaque model for NFS seems to indicate that your write data
> blocks to the server, and read them back.  Is this format only
> for text files?  So programs running on RSX can read disk blocks
> directly and process accordingly?  That would allow for conversion
> programs to be written for either side.

Oh sure. If you aren't really interested in NFS as a file system, but 
just as a file transportation device, then sure. But why use NFS in that 
case. FTP will work equally fine.

In RSX, programs *always* read/write disk blocks. There is nothing else. 
All else are code that exist inside the user program.

>>>> I could go on about binary files as well, where you can hit 
>>>> the same problems...  
>>>> And then we have things like owners and file protections, 
>>>> which don't map right...
> 
> Owner and file protection is also well known.  (Especially for
> DOS which has none of either.)  In some cases special servers
> exist to do the conversion, but otherwise NFS works with numeric
> UID and GID and the usual unix rwx protections.  As well as I
> remember, the rwx idea came from DEC systems, so might work 
> with RSX.

Exist, yes. Map right, no. In RSX, user and group identities are only 8 
bits. And you have four access bits for each category, not 3 like in 
Unix. And some of those bits have meanings that don't translate.
And user and group are tightly connected in RSX. A user is actually 
identified by the user and group value as a combination. You can have 
different users with the same user field value, but different group 
values. They are different users in RSX, unlike in Unix.

You could say that uids (in RSX) are gid local.

>>> If it is done in the file system before that limitation occurs,
>>> then it shouldn't be a problem.  I don't know the RSX file 
