Yes, things changed over the years as memory size increased.

The MVS file usual system is still pretty much that from OS/360.
S/360 (the hardware, distinguished from OS/360 software) disk 
drives allow the user to specify the block size.  There is no
fixed size (like 512), but anything from 1 to the length of
a disk track is allowed.  It is convenient for small memory
systems in that buffers only need to be as big as disk blocks.
Small memory systems use smaller block sizes (80 is popular).
Larger systems, for higher performace, use larger block sizes.

Unlike unix, where the system buffers data blocks, the OS/360
model writes data directly from the disk into user buffers.
Among other features of OS/360 is locate mode I/O.  When reading,
data goes directly into the desired location.  For writing, 
the I/O system supplies a buffer address, data is written directly
into the buffer, and then it is scheduled to be written.

In days past, it saved memory and was faster.  It still seems
useful in avoiding one copy of the data, even on more
modern systems.

