What some IBM C systems do is, instead of supplying the byte
offset as the return from ftell(), they return 
(record number)*32768+(offset into record).   (The usual files
structures don't allow records larger than 32767 bytes.)
They do that for text files, and for RECFM=V (variable length
records).  The OS/360 (and successor) file system keeps track
of blocks but not bytes.  In the case of fixed length records
(blocked or not), it is easy to find the block number and byte
offset in normal conditions.  Even so, there is one complication.
The last block of a file can be smaller than the rest.
(It will still be a multiple of the record length, though.)
If you append to a file, new blocks are added after the short
block, such that record calculations can be wrong.  For RECFM=V,
the blocks are packed together as allowed by the disk hardware,
again complicating the calculation of block number.
 
