that isn't true, and also that you can't just do sequential
access until you find EOF, yes you have to go through the extra work.
 
> All to fulfill the very restrictive definition on how 
> fseek/ftell are assured to work in C.
 
(snip)

>>> Ok. So totall non-interoperability over NFS. What should I do with files 
>>> that were created on one side, when they are accessed on the other side?
>>> Pretend they don't exist? That can cause interesting problems of its own.
 
>> It seems that there are some "pretend they don't exist" problems
>> already.  One comes with case sensitivity.  If the server has two
>> files with names that differ only in case, how can they be
>> accessed by a case insensitive client (DOS/Windows or Macintosh,
>> including OS X)?  Or the name may be too long for the client.
 
> Indeed. But should I do that for *all* files, just because I might have 
> to do it for some?

As I said, my choice is conversion programs only when using the
other file format.  Much of the time data will only be read
back on the same system that wrote it, and all is fine.
 
>>> Pretend they are fixed length files? But NFS (and Unix) measure file 
>>> length in bytes, while RSX measure it in blocks. Or should I pretend 
>>> they are STREAM files? Which makes them rather difficult to play with, 
>>> but which might be more approriate, except when we really talk about 
>>> binary files, which might make sense to say that they are fixed length 
>>> files.
 
>> The opaque model for NFS seems to indicate that your write data
>> blocks to the server, and read them back.  Is this format only
>> for text files?  So programs running on RSX can read disk blocks
>> directly and process accordingly?  That would allow for conversion
>> programs to be written for either side.
 
> Oh sure. If you aren't really interested in NFS as a file system, but 
> just as a file transportation device, then sure. But why use NFS in that 
> case. FTP will work equally fine.

Well, ftp still has some of the problems, but yes that does help.
 
> In RSX, programs *always* read/write disk blocks. There is nothing else. 
> All else are code that exist inside the user program.

Then again, for ftp you need TCP, where you only need UDP
to do NFS.  (Well, there is tftp...)
 
(snip)
>> Owner and file protection is also well known.  (Especially for
>> DOS which has none of either.)  In some cases special servers
>> exist to do the conversion, but otherwise NFS works with numeric
>> UID and GID and the usual unix rwx protections.  As well as I
>> remember, the rwx idea came from DEC systems, so might work 
>> with RSX.
 
> Exist, yes. Map right, no. In RSX, user and group identities are only 8 
> bits. And you have four access bits for each category, not 3 like in 
> Unix. And some of those bits have meanings that don't translate.
> And user and group are tightly connected in RSX. A user is actually 
> identified by the user and group value as a combination. You can have 
> different users with the same user field value, but different group 
> values. They are different users in RSX, unlike in Unix.

As explained in some of those documents, they don't even all
translate unix to unix.  One unix feature is the ability to
open a file and then unlink (rm or delete) it.  The file stays
open and usable until it is closed by the last program with
it open.  That doesn't translate well to a stateless NFS server,
so a unix NFS client actual does a rename instead of unlink
to a temporary (hidden) file name.  Normally, the client will
remove the file when it is finally closed, but if the client
crashes before that, the file will be left on the server.

I belive that another case relates to the read and execute bits,
in that a file can have execute but not read access.  As the
NFS server has no way to know what the client is doing with
the bits, you can read such a file.  It seems, then, that one
maps protection bits as well as one can, but not always
perfectly.
 
> You could say that uids (in RSX) are gid local.

NFS matches files by numeric UID and GID.  That assumes
that the all users have the same UID and GID on all client systems.
Even more, it assumes that there are UID and GID.  I believe
that the PCNFS clients had special servers to get around that,
I am not sure what Windows does.  (At least NT has user IDs.)
 
>>>> If it is done in the file system before that limitation occurs,
>>>> then it shouldn't be a problem.  I don't know the RSX file 
>>>> access code at all, though.  There is, as I said, the problem of
>>>> programs not allowing for enough characters in a file name.
  
>>> The filename mangling is done before the ACP is ever hit. It's all 
>>> inside the user program code. No chance of catching it before that. In 
>>> many cases, it's even mangled at compilation time.
 
>> I believe this is an old problem, too.  Consider, for example, 
>> the 8.3 file name mapping done by windows.  Ugly, but it does work.
 
> Like I said from the start - I don't consider filename issues to be a 
> problem. That part is easy...
> But since you brought it up, I thought I'd just explain a little about 
> how it works in RSX.

