FPGAs are amazing things, I've had a brief tinker with Xilinx Spartan
stuff in recent months. Starting from scratch with a bare FPGA chip
and a hand-built prototype is verging on the insane (and certainly not
very productive) unless you have good reason to enjoy high precision
surface mount soldering and the like. Classical wire-wrap-style
prototyping may not be appropriate. Instead, amateurs might prefer to
pick one of the variety of ready made FPGA development cards with
various quantities of configurable IO, memory, etc, which are
available from companies like Digilent (US based, but widely
available) from around $100 or so depending on capabilities, and
connect *that* to your signals of interest.

A "production" design without the "development" stuff (switches, LEDs,
extra header pins, maybe USB, etc) around it could cost substantially
less than that (though maybe still a few dozen dollars in consumer
quantities).

A more than adequate (for this kind of thing) flavour of Xilinx's
development tools is freely downloadable (Webpack ISE).

The Internerd finds many people who have started things like PDP11s in
FPGA. There seem to be not so many who have finished the job.

For introductory FPGA info, www.fpga4fun.com (yes, honestly) is worth
a look.


