OK, a few very quick pointers.

First, I am not sure if/why you want to connect a console to the SROM
port. If I remember rightly, the SROM port expects a loader program,
not a human on a terminal. The mini-debugger talks to the usual
console (nb please verify with mini-debugger docs below as this is
unchecked and from a LONG time ago).

PWS is EV5/21164, whereas AlphaStation 200/400 doc mentioned above is
EV4/21064. Maybe you knew that, maybe it doesn't matter for this.

When the SROM console starts, it is (according to the SROM
minidebugger manual) silent till it has had some input it can use for
autobaud.

A User Guide for the SROM mini-debugger can still be found at:
http://www.compaq.com/cpq-alphaserver/technology/literature/srommini.pdf

A Technical Reference Manual for the original PC164 evaluation boards
(which have a fair amount in common with PWS) can still be found at:
http://www.compaq.com/cpq-alphaserver/technology/literature/pc164trm.pdf
Sadly it does not seem to contain the SROM test port pinout.

A manual which does contain an SROM test port pinout (see section 4.9)
can be found at:
http://www.alphalinux.org/firmware/api/LX/LX_USER.pdf
But I can't confirm the pinout on the PC164LX is the same as on the
PWS, and it does not match the pinout you posted earlier (your
AlphaStation 200/400 was 10 pin, the PC164LX is 6 pin).

Not sure where this gets you, but good luck anyway.


