I'm actually still working on the card Rob mentioned (slowly, though,
because I have a $6500 tax bill and a wedding coming up plus grad
school and... well, these things build up).  I design embedded systems
by day which include lots of FPGAs, so I'm fairly familiar with the
task.

The bottom line is that in production quantities for this type of
audience (i.e. I could probably get away with making 100 ever, but
probably only as much as 25 at a time if I had a lot of money to blow
building them), the boards cost $200-$250 a pop to build correctly.
That includes the PCB and assembly cost, which make up generally 1/3
of the total (though the PCB cost goes down dramatically as the
quantity goes up; they're about $87 each in quantity 10, but about $10
in quantity 100).

But yeah, FPGAs are amazing things.  My advice: don't stop at Xilinx.
Their Spartan 6 is the first actually compelling FPGA I've seen from
them in years, whereas Altera has been making fantastic devices with
fewer bugs for a long time.  Also, Altera's software is leaps and
bounds ahead, and they have very nice eval boards; my personal
favorite is the one they sell as the "Cyclone II Starter Kit" for $150
(it's a design Terasic did for them called the DE2).  Decent amounts
of SDRAM and flash, SD slot, VGA out, RS232, etc. with the programmer
built in!  If you're looking to start somewhere, I'd say that's an
ideal board (I liked it so much I have two).


