With even a fairly small (these days) FPGA you could do a lot
on a Qbus board.  Add on a few serial ports, a few megabytes
of SRAM, even a floppy disk controller.

Some that are common on the starter boards are PS2 keyboard
or mouse connector, serial ports, and VGA port.  They are
just buffers or resistors from the FPGA pins.

You could probably fit the whole PDP-11 CPU into a medium
sized FPGA.

As for bus buffers, presumably one would want the full drive
of the DEC buffers for a full system, but it might be that
easier to obtain drivers would work well for the systems
that most of us have available.  

As I was recently interested in an S100 project, I did find
that the 8T97 is available, though somewhat more expensive
than the more usual 74367.

