On Apr 9, 4:02=A0pm, John Wallace <johnwalla...@yahoo.co.uk> wrote:
> Am I right in thinking that the original Qbus spec allowed for
> multiple daisy-chained enclosures? Or was that just for Unibus?
> Certainly multiple enclosures introduces a variety of issues re drive
> strength and timing and the like.
>
> If the multiple enclosures constraint was originally present and now
> can conveniently be ignored, I suspect that the question of drivers
> gets a whole lot simpler. The FPGAs I have been tinkering with have
> programmable IO voltages, programmable drive strengths, and
> programmable slew rates, so a rough approximation might not even need
> an external transceiver, in the single-box case.

The problem you'll find with modern FPGAs is that they generally only
support 3.3v max on their I/Os (more than that and you could easily
blow it up).  QBUS sits at something closer, but with the inevitable
ringing you'll see on the bus, it peaks somewhat higher.  Thus the
need for an open-collector bridge like 74AS641 or 757.

Also, QBUS specs give a needed drive strength of 64 mA.  The most I
ever see in the FPGAs I work with is about 24 mA, which would probably
be enough to successfully drive something; however, I'd always feel
more comfortable with external buffers (not least because they're
cheaper and easier to replace than an FPGA).

- Dave


