Bill Gunshannon wrote:
> In article <nGp8w9et44Sm@eisner.encompasserve.org>,
> According to the designer IDE does not require a fancy controller as
> it is included with the disk.
> 
> For those who might still be interested in this, here are pictures and a
> write-up.
>  
> http://www.cs.uofs.edu/~bill/M4ide.pdf
> http://www.cs.uofs.edu/~bill/M4ide.big.pdf
> 
> Note that it consists of 3 simple IC's and a little glue.

A few years ago one of the Russian posters to comp.os.vms posted a link 
to his schematic for an IDE to QBUS adapter.  He even had a driver for 
one of the older versions of VAX/VMS.

However, you have some new challenges.

The old ATA interface is basically obsolete, to use the full capacity of 
the newer drives, apparently you need to support UDMA mode, not the mode 
you have the plans for.

Theorectically a UDMA ATA driver would allow a DS10 to access more than 
137 GB on the newer drives.  But I do not know anyone that is working on 
that.  And it is unknown if the effective IDE bus lengths on the various 
Alpha servers is short enough to support UDMA.

I am expecting ATA devices to also become hard to find as they will be 
replaced with SATA.

So if you want your project to have some longevity, you would want to 
use SATA drives.  In one case, this makes it more complex, but if you 
just make a gateway to a single board micro computer with a real time OS 
that supports SATA.

My personal plan is to just set up SIM-H VAX on a XEN Linux partition 
and use it to MSCP serve some large SATA disks.  Then I can use either 
Linux mirroring or VMS Shadowing to have redundant storage.

It would only be eithernet speed, but that should be good enough for 
hobby work.

-John
