better with time.

>> But if you would do one, a lot of people would be interested. If you 
>> don't implement some existing DEC controller interface on the PDP-11 
>> side, no existing boot roms will be able to boot it. No existing device 
>> drivers will be able to access it, and so on... Less than ideal...
> 
> My intent with IDE was not to emulate anything.  It was to create a
> raw interface to the IDE disk just like was done on the other micros
> I have seen this done for (at least 5 that I know of).  Writing boot
> roms and device drivers would be part of the game.  It's not like
> most of us are doing this for a living.  I see it as an academic
> exercise that, if successful, would also provide me with a very useful
> resource as I have a number of PDP-11 and VAX systems and a dwindling
> supply of disks that I can hook up to them.

Then I totally fail to understand the IDE - ST-506 comment above. Why 
are you involving ST-506 anywhere?

Also, yes, sure, this can be done. Will it actually be done? I doubt it. 
Writing the device drivers and so on, will be very tricky to say the 
least. Yes, I can whip together a device driver for RSX. But it will not 
be able to boot from it. There is very specialized code located in other 
places that are also needed for a bootable device, and not all of those 
parts comes with source code.
And writing boot proms might also be a headache, since we don't have the 
sources to those either, and there is a lot of code in them, and some of 
that you want to keep around. And boot proms comes in different formats 
depending on the system.

I will definitely not stop you from doing this, but my suspicion is that 
it will end up being just a disk controller that proves that you can 
build a disk controller, but it will not actually be usable for any of 
the systems mentioned.

That's why I keep saying that if you want something actually usable, you 
should probably go with emulating an existing controller. And of those, 
MSCP is the only one with the properties needed to actually make it 
meaningful.

Just my $.02.

