In article <hps7i9$htt$1@naig.caltech.edu>,
	glen herrmannsfeldt <gah@ugcs.caltech.edu> writes:
> In alt.sys.pdp11 Bill Gunshannon <billg999@cs.uofs.edu> wrote:
> 
> (snip, I wrote)
>>> One problem that some have is the 16 bit data bus.  While the early
>>> IDE allowed for either 8 or 16 bit transfers, the 8 bit ability
>>> disappeared pretty fast.  For Qbus presumably you won't have
>>> that problem.
>  
>> I believe this was discussed in the write-up I saw for one of 
>> them and there were ways around it.
> 
> Yes, it requires an extra eight bit latch.  When you read
> the first byte it latches the other half of the bus. 
> Then you read that latch, probably from a different I/O port.
> 
> (snip)
>  
>> The one for the Z80 based Tandy micros cost about $8.
> 
> That includes the extra latch.  For Qbus, can you require 16 bit
> transfers?  If you write the device driver and boot ROM it
> would seem that you could do that.  
> 
> Actually, I was wondering some time ago about interfacing
> to the ISA bus.  That takes a little more buffering, but
> then allows more different devices to be connected.
> 
> PCI is likely somewhat harder, but allows for newer devices.

I think both of these probably go beyond the realm of possibilty for
a hobbyist implementation.  By the time you got done the card would
cost more than an entire PDP-11 or VAX system.

> 
> (snip of my suggestion to do ethernet and NFS)
>  
>> Yeah, that's an option, but likely to be much harder and more costly.
> 
> Well, ethernet Qbus cards aren't so hard to find.  I believe they
> are more reasonably priced than SCSI cards.  

I have a number of them both QBUS and UNIBUS.

>                                               You could connect
> that to a SAN disk supporting your favorite protocol.

That would require the complete implementation of an IP stack.
Considering the limited resources of a PDP-11, I would expect
it to be nearly impossible.

I would be more likely to look at a very simple ethernet level protocol
that merely knew how to fetch and return raw blocks with a server
process on another machine.  Or maybe that is something that could be
done with LAT.  Is LAT what PDP-11's use to boot over ethernet?

> 
> The driver is somewhat more complicated, though.

If it requires a full IP stack, yeah, a bit more complicated.  :-)

bill

